WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:09.360
When we see this work for the first time,&nbsp;
we might think it is a charming scene.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:14.160
A little girl walks along the edge of a&nbsp;
meadow at sunset. She wears a yellow dress,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:20.400
a white bonnet, and in her hands she holds a&nbsp;
bouquet of wildflowers she has just picked.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:25.200
The sky is tinted with pinks and&nbsp;
oranges. The field is full of flowers.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:29.200
It is a tender, peaceful image...&nbsp;
Until you look to the right.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:34.960
Walking along the path, right beside the&nbsp;
girl, there is a skeletal figure wrapped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:39.680
in rags. It moves forward slowly,&nbsp;
leaning on two wooden crutches,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:45.440
with a black veil fluttering in the wind&nbsp;
behind it. And from its arm hangs a sickle.

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:48.320
It is Death. And the girl has no idea it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:53.120
there... or maybe she does. That&nbsp;
is the mystery of this painting.

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:55.600
Death and the Girl was painted at the end of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:00.000
nineteenth century by a Belgian&nbsp;
artist named Frans Van Kuyck.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.680
It is a painting with very little documentation.&nbsp;
We do not know with certainty the exact year it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:11.280
was created, nor do we know the history of its&nbsp;
owners. But that has not stopped it from becoming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:15.920
in recent years, one of the most shared&nbsp;
and commented images on the internet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:19.040
often presented as a deeply disturbing picture.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:24.480
And when we analyze it carefully, it is&nbsp;
understandable why it generates that feeling.

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:29.920
Let us think for a moment about how Death has&nbsp;
been represented throughout the history of art.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:34.480
On this channel we have already seen&nbsp;
several versions. The Death that watches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:40.160
Life in Klimt's work. The Death that plays&nbsp;
the violin into Böcklin's ear. The medieval&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:45.120
Death of the Danse Macabre, dancing&nbsp;
away with kings and peasants alike.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:52.160
In almost all of those representations, Death is&nbsp;
powerful. It is painted as an imposing, active,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:58.160
and dominant force. However, the Death in this&nbsp;
painting is something completely different.

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:03.600
Death here walks with difficulty, leaning&nbsp;
on two wooden crutches. Its skeletal body&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:07.840
is wrapped in tattered, worn-out&nbsp;
clothing. Its posture is hunched,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:11.520
like that of someone exhausted.&nbsp;
And instead of chasing the girl,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:17.200
it simply walks beside her, at the same&nbsp;
pace, like an old traveling companion.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:20.160
Now let us look at the details of the composition.

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:23.680
Van Kuyck painted this girl at&nbsp;
the brightest point of the scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:28.240
with her yellow dress and white bonnet&nbsp;
reflecting the last light of the day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:33.280
We might think she represents life in&nbsp;
its purest form: childhood, innocence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:37.760
the beginning of everything. And she is&nbsp;
completely absorbed in her little world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:43.600
contemplating the flowers she carries in her&nbsp;
hands, entirely unaware of what walks beside her.

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:47.120
The flowers are perhaps the most&nbsp;
unsettling detail in the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:51.840
painting. The image invites us to imagine&nbsp;
that the girl has spent the afternoon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:56.080
picking flowers from the field. Pulling&nbsp;
them from the ground where they grew,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:01.200
to take them with her. And this is directly&nbsp;
related to the sickle the skeleton holds.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:04.640
A sickle is commonly used to cut down what grows.

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:09.360
Without showing it explicitly, the artist&nbsp;
paints the two figures in this painting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:14.640
doing exactly the same thing. While the&nbsp;
girl cuts flowers, Death cuts lives.

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:20.480
And the flowers the girl holds so tenderly are,&nbsp;
in reality, a reminder that every life pulled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:26.080
from its soil, however beautiful, begins&nbsp;
to wither at the exact moment it is cut.

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:30.160
Finally, there is the sunset. The&nbsp;
scene does not take place in broad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:33.840
daylight nor in the darkness of&nbsp;
night. It happens at the exact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:38.480
moment of transition. When the light is&nbsp;
fading and the night is approaching. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:44.000
day coming to an end as a metaphor for&nbsp;
life moving, inevitably, toward its own.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:48.080
And now it is worth talking a little&nbsp;
about the artist. Because his story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:52.160
adds a completely unexpected&nbsp;
layer of mystery to this work.

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:55.520
But before doing so, I would like&nbsp;
to remind you that if you would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:59.840
like to collect this painting, it&nbsp;
is now available in our catalog.

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:04.240
On our website you will find more&nbsp;
than 1,000 carefully selected works&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:08.160
so you can choose your favorites&nbsp;
and begin your own art collection.

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:12.320
Shipping is free worldwide, and by&nbsp;
purchasing from the website you will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:17.040
be supporting me enormously in continuing&nbsp;
to create videos like this every week.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:21.200
As I told you at the beginning of this&nbsp;
video, Frans Van Kuyck was a Belgian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:28.720
artist born in 1854 in the city of Antwerp.&nbsp;
This man grew up in a family of artists. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:34.320
grandfather was a painter. His father was a&nbsp;
painter. And he himself studied at the Royal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:39.360
Academy of Fine Arts in his city, where&nbsp;
years later he would become a professor.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:42.880
But Van Kuyck was not only&nbsp;
a painter. He was also one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:47.360
of the most important public figures&nbsp;
in Antwerp. He was a city councilor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:52.160
Alderman of Fine Arts, and a member&nbsp;
of the boards of several museums.

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:55.280
Curiously, and you can tell&nbsp;
me if this is a coincidence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.280 --> 00:05:01.040
Van Kuyck is remembered in Belgium for two&nbsp;
things that seem contradictory. On one hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:05.600
he was one of the people responsible for&nbsp;
establishing Mother's Day in his country.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:10.560
And on the other hand, it was thanks to his&nbsp;
initiative that the city of Antwerp acquired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:16.160
the Schoonselhof cemetery, which remains one&nbsp;
of the most important in Belgium to this day.

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:21.520
We could say, then, that the man who painted Death&nbsp;
walking beside a little girl was the same man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:28.400
who gave his country a holiday to celebrate life,&nbsp;
and his city a place to bid farewell to its dead.

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:33.360
In fact, this painting could be closely&nbsp;
related to the maternal figure. If we look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:38.800
at Death in this painting, its figure resembles&nbsp;
a feminine presence more than a masculine one;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:42.720
and although we have no context about&nbsp;
the true meaning of its presence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:47.840
we could imagine it as a mother accompanying&nbsp;
her daughter along the path of life.

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:50.480
As I told you at the beginning, we have absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:54.720
no way of knowing whether this girl&nbsp;
is aware that Death walks beside her.

00:05:54.720 --> 00:06:00.480
But let us notice something. The girl shows no&nbsp;
sign of fear. On the contrary, she walks calmly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:06.240
contemplating her flowers, with the serenity of&nbsp;
someone who feels accompanied rather than pursued.

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:10.960
And if we look at Death, the black veil&nbsp;
fluttering in the wind recalls the mourning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:17.280
veil worn by women of that era. Its hunched&nbsp;
posture and crutches evoke a tired old woman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:19.920
and its head is slightly turned toward the girl,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:23.920
as if watching to make sure she does&nbsp;
not stray too far from the path.

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:28.640
Under this interpretation, death should&nbsp;
be seen not as the end that awaits us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:33.520
but as a presence that accompanies and&nbsp;
watches over us throughout our lives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:38.800
walking beside us with patience, until&nbsp;
the moment comes to take us by the hand.

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:41.920
And coming from the man who&nbsp;
gave his country Mother's Day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:46.400
that may very well have been the idea.

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:49.760
I would love to know if you share&nbsp;
this interpretation of the painting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:54.480
or if you have another way of seeing this&nbsp;
work, please share it in the comments.

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:58.240
As always, thank you so much for&nbsp;
watching the video until the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:04.240
and I will see you in the next video.

